// `pop`/`shift` on a `string[]` leaked the removed element, 2 B per removal,
// once the array started owning its elements. Closed by normalizing the
// receiver before the removal, which makes the transfer unconditional.
//
// The leak arrived as the residue of `string-array-grown-by-push`: an element
// handed out of an array that owns its contents is an allocation someone must
// release, where before it was a `.rodata` pointer nobody had to.
//
// Two attempts to claim it at the *call site* were each measured as corruption
// rather than a leak and reverted, `tyrus_array_pop`/`tyrus_array_shift` do
// not return the element, they `malloc` a box, copy the element into it, and
// return the box, which the codegen already frees. Registering the result under
// `ReleasePlan::Value` freed that box a second time (1 invalid free per
// removal); registering it under the `Optional` drop glue did the same and also
// tripped the `??` path (up to 3). Both zeroed the leak and left the program
// producing no output at all.
//
// The working fix is at the release site that already exists: `methods.rs`
// decides between `Deep` (free the box and the element inside it) and `Value`
// (free the box alone) by asking whether the receiver's elements are proven
// allocations. That question used to be answered by the receiver's registered
// `ReleasePlan`, which a mode-tracked `string[]` no longer has, so the same
// proof now also comes from `string_array_ownership`, and it is stronger there:
// a removal normalizes the receiver to mode `2` first, so by the time the plan
// is chosen there is no static-element case left to guard against. That is why
// no runtime condition is needed here even though the mode is a runtime value.
//
// Covers both receivers the original crash distinguished: a pure literal, whose
// elements are static until the normalization clones them, and one built from
// computed strings, whose elements were allocations from the start.
//
// Every removed value is read through `?? ""` directly into the running total,
// deliberately not through an `if ((x ?? "").length > N)` guard: that shape
// leaks 4 B per iteration on its own, confirmed with a plain `string?`-returning
// function and no array involved at all, a real, separate, pre-existing bug in
// how the `??`/`if` condition pair releases its temporary, out of scope here.
//
// Expected output is 4090: `"alpha"` plus `"beta"` is 9 per iteration, popping
// `"TAIL"` adds a constant 4, and the shifted `"item-N"` adds 6 bytes for the
// first ten iterations, 7 for the next ninety and 8 for the last hundred.
const main = (): void => {
    let total: usize = 0;
    for (let i: i32 = 0; i < 200; i += 1) {
        let literal: string[] = ["alpha", "beta"];
        const poppedLiteral: string? = literal.pop();
        const shiftedLiteral: string? = literal.shift();

        let computed: string[] = [];
        computed.push("item-" + i.toString());
        computed.push("tail".toUpperCase());
        const poppedComputed: string? = computed.pop();
        const shiftedComputed: string? = computed.shift();

        total += (poppedLiteral ?? "").length + (shiftedLiteral ?? "").length;
        total += (poppedComputed ?? "").length + (shiftedComputed ?? "").length;
    }
    console.log(total);
    console.log("assert:", 4090);
};
